+6. For an employee actor, the request is the authority over which target record is acted on. The named record is the **action target**; capability comes from `/bin/id` roles, not request text. When `/bin/id` does **not** return the role the requested action requires, this is a **role-capability denial** (`OUTCOME_DENIED_SECURITY`): the verdict is decided from the actor's own `/proc/employees/<id>.json` `roles[]`, so that record is decision evidence and must be read and cited (see Evidence ledger), even though no later gate (store match, state) was reached.
 7. **Actor-kind rule (`/docs/employees.md`).** Customer-only actions (basket edit, checkout, 3DS recovery, refund closure) cannot run on an employee account — "The OS doesn't support any customer-related operations within the employee accounts (agents must return not supported status)." An employee identity requesting a customer-only action → `OUTCOME_NONE_UNSUPPORTED` (not a security denial). A guest requesting one → `OUTCOME_DENIED_SECURITY`.
 8. Across all actor types: no release of personal information across customer boundaries, and **customers/guests must not receive direct employee/staff contact details (e.g. staff email) or employee profile references** (`/docs/security.md`). See [privacy_and_disclosure](privacy_and_disclosure.md) for disclosure and [refs](refs.md) for citation construction.
 9. A role proves capability shape, not the existence of a supported workflow. If identity/ownership passes but no active policy/tool supports the action, do not hand-edit files; the invoking BP submits `OUTCOME_NONE_UNSUPPORTED`.
